/* ============================================================================
nth-prime.epher -- the n-th prime number

Counts primes until the counter reaches n, with a packed while loop
(guide 1.30). The state holds {candidate, primes counted}; each pass
checks the next candidate and moves on, and the loop stops the moment
the n-th prime has been counted. Checks: the 10th prime is 29, the
25th is 97, and the 100th is 541 (Hardy and Wright, ch. 1, tables).

Source: standard number theory; the count is exact because isprime is
exact. The loop runs about n log n steps, so keep n below a few
thousand for an instant run.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/primes/nth-prime.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\primes\nth-prime.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/primes/nth-prime.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

The one knob to edit is `n` below.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
const n = 25

// ---- count up to the n-th prime -------------------------------------------------
// v = {candidate, primes counted}; the pass checks candidate + 1, so when
// the count reaches n the loop leaves v[1] on the prime that did it.
v = {1, 0}
while v[2] < n do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1] + 1) then 1 else 0)}
print("the " + str(n) + "th prime:", v[1])

// ---- the classic checks ----------------------------------------------------------
// The same loop shape against the table values for n = 10 and n = 100.
v = {1, 0}
while v[2] < 10 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1] + 1) then 1 else 0)}
print("the 10th prime:", v[1])
v = {1, 0}
while v[2] < 100 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if isprime(v[1] + 1) then 1 else 0)}
print("the 100th prime:", v[1])
/* ---- expected output ----
= 25
= {1, 0}
= the 25th prime: 97
= {1, 0}
= the 10th prime: 29
= {1, 0}
= the 100th prime: 541
*/
